<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/expert/macroeconomic-models-dsge">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    DSGE models — <strong>Dynamic, Stochastic, General Equilibrium</strong> — are how modern
    macroeconomics thinks formally: simulated economies built from optimising households and
    firms, hit by random shocks, with sticky prices that let policy bite. They emerged after the
    1970s "rational expectations revolution" demolished older models, and they are the engines
    inside every serious central bank — though their failures in 2008 made the field
    appropriately humble.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the DSGE skeleton, from microfoundations to central-bank use" widget="equationStep" config={dsgeCfg} />

  <h2>The honest scorecard</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> internal consistency (no unexplained free parameters), clear policy counterfactuals, explicit assumptions you can argue with — an enormous advance on curves fitted to history.</li>
    <li><strong>The 2008 critique:</strong> standard DSGE models had no meaningful banking sector, no house prices, no debt — the crisis hit exactly where the models were blind. Queen Elizabeth asked economists why nobody saw it; the models were part of the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>The response:</strong> financial-friction DSGE (Gertler & Kiyotaki), heterogeneous-agent models (HANK — households with different wealth, which changes everything about who spends a stimulus), and hybrid empirical models at central banks.</li>
    <li><strong>The pluralist turn:</strong> forecasting now leans on big-data machine learning; structural claims still need DSGE-style theory. The mature position is an ensemble — no single model earns full trust.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kydland & Prescott (Nobel 2004); Smets & Wouters (2007, the benchmark model); HANK: Kaplan, Moll & Violante (2018).</p>
</TopicPage>
